---

# 14. Assumptions

The project is based on the following assumptions:

1. HR stakeholders will provide timely business clarification.
2. PostgreSQL will remain the transactional database for the current scope.
3. Authorized users will access the application through supported web browsers.
4. Approved business rules will be consistently implemented across backend and frontend layers.
5. Power BI will consume approved analytics/data outputs.
6. Git/GitHub will be used for source-code version control.
7. Business stakeholders will participate in UAT and requirement validation.

---

# 15. Project Constraints

The project is subject to the following constraints:

* Initial implementation is a portfolio/project environment rather than a production enterprise deployment.
* The application stack does not use Python.
* Initial scope focuses on core HR operations rather than full payroll/ERP functionality.
* External integrations may require additional technical and security assessment.
* Several project delivery roles are consolidated under one project team member.

---

# 16. Key Project Risks

| Risk ID | Risk                                     | Impact                  | Mitigation                                   |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| R-001   | Changing business requirements           | Rework                  | Backlog refinement and change control        |
| R-002   | Inconsistent business rules              | Incorrect results       | Centralized business-rule documentation      |
| R-003   | Poor data quality                        | Incorrect analytics     | Validation and database constraints          |
| R-004   | Frontend/API integration issues          | Incomplete workflows    | API mapping and integration testing          |
| R-005   | KPI definitions not aligned              | Reporting inconsistency | KPI definition and stakeholder review        |
| R-006   | Insufficient UAT                         | Post-release defects    | Scenario-based UAT                           |
| R-007   | Documentation falling behind development | Traceability gaps       | Documentation included in Definition of Done |

---

# 17. Success Criteria

The project will be considered successful when:

1. Core HR processes are documented and represented in the solution.
2. PostgreSQL is successfully integrated with the backend.
3. REST APIs are available for the approved HR modules.
4. Department-specific active-status rules produce expected results.
5. Employee, attendance, leave, performance, training and recruitment data can be managed through the system.
6. Frontend workflows consume the approved backend APIs.
7. Management KPIs are traceable to defined data sources and business rules.
8. Testing and UAT documentation is completed.
9. Requirements can be traced through the RTM.
10. Project source code and documentation are maintained through Git/GitHub.

